{"subjectId":"java","id":"java-dsa-cheatsheet","title":"Java for DSA: Collections Cheat Sheet","group":"Design, Modern Java & Interview Prep","order":74,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The complexities, idioms and templates you reach for in every coding interview, in Java.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Complexity cheat sheet</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Access</th><th>Search</th><th>Insert</th><th>Delete</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int[]</code> / array</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Fixed size</td></tr><tr><td><code>ArrayList</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1) amortised at end</td><td>O(n) (shift)</td><td>Default list</td></tr><tr><td><code>LinkedList</code></td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1) at ends</td><td>O(1) at ends</td><td>Rarely the best choice</td></tr><tr><td><code>ArrayDeque</code></td><td>—</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1) both ends</td><td>O(1) both ends</td><td>Stack <strong>and</strong> queue</td></tr><tr><td><code>HashMap</code> / <code>HashSet</code></td><td>—</td><td>O(1) avg</td><td>O(1) avg</td><td>O(1) avg</td><td>Worst case O(log n) with treeified bins</td></tr><tr><td><code>TreeMap</code> / <code>TreeSet</code></td><td>—</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>Sorted; floor/ceiling queries</td></tr><tr><td><code>PriorityQueue</code></td><td>peek O(1)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>poll O(log n)</td><td>Binary min-heap</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hash\"></i>Maps: counting and grouping</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map&lt;Character, Integer&gt; freq = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();          // sorted keys for display\n        for (char c : \"banana\".toCharArray()) {\n            freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);                       // add 1 or start at 1\n        }\n        System.out.println(freq);\n\n        int[] counts = new int[26];                                // faster for lowercase letters\n        for (char c : \"banana\".toCharArray()) counts[c - 'a']++;\n        System.out.println(counts['a' - 'a'] + \" \" + counts['n' - 'a']);\n\n        Map&lt;Integer, List&lt;String&gt;&gt; byLength = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();\n        for (String w : List.of(\"hi\", \"cat\", \"go\", \"dog\")) {\n            byLength.computeIfAbsent(w.length(), k -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(w);\n        }\n        System.out.println(byLength);\n        System.out.println(freq.getOrDefault('z', 0));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{a=3, b=1, n=2}\n3 2\n{2=[hi, go], 3=[cat, dog]}\n0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-push\"></i>Stack, queue and heap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        stack.push(1); stack.push(2); stack.push(3);\n        System.out.println(stack.pop() + \" \" + stack.peek());      // LIFO\n\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; queue = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        queue.offer(1); queue.offer(2); queue.offer(3);\n        System.out.println(queue.poll() + \" \" + queue.peek());     // FIFO\n\n        PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; minHeap = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(List.of(5, 1, 4));\n        PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; maxHeap = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.reverseOrder());\n        maxHeap.addAll(List.of(5, 1, 4));\n        System.out.println(minHeap.poll() + \" \" + maxHeap.poll());\n\n        // heap of int[] pairs ordered by the second value\n        PriorityQueue&lt;int[]&gt; pq = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;((x, y) -&gt; Integer.compare(x[1], y[1]));\n        pq.add(new int[]{7, 30}); pq.add(new int[]{8, 10}); pq.add(new int[]{9, 20});\n        System.out.println(pq.poll()[0]);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 2\n1 2\n1 5\n8</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending-numbers\"></i>TreeMap: ordered queries</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeMap&lt;Integer, String&gt; tm = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;(Map.of(10, \"a\", 20, \"b\", 30, \"c\"));\n        System.out.println(tm.floorKey(25) + \" \" + tm.ceilingKey(25));    // ≤ 25, ≥ 25\n        System.out.println(tm.lowerKey(10) + \" \" + tm.higherKey(30));     // strict: none → null\n        System.out.println(tm.firstKey() + \" \" + tm.lastEntry());\n        System.out.println(tm.headMap(20) + \" \" + tm.tailMap(20, true));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>20 30\nnull null\n10 30=c\n{10=a} {20=b, 30=c}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>Templates</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int lowerBound(int[] a, int target) {        // first index with a[i] &gt;= target\n        int lo = 0, hi = a.length;\n        while (lo &lt; hi) {\n            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;                // not (lo + hi) / 2 — may overflow\n            if (a[mid] &lt; target) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;\n        }\n        return lo;\n    }\n\n    static int shortestPath(char[][] grid) {            // BFS on a grid, '#' = wall\n        int rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;\n        int[][] dist = new int[rows][cols];\n        for (int[] row : dist) Arrays.fill(row, -1);\n        Deque&lt;int[]&gt; q = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        q.offer(new int[]{0, 0});\n        dist[0][0] = 0;\n        int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) {\n            int[] cur = q.poll();\n            for (int[] d : dirs) {\n                int r = cur[0] + d[0], c = cur[1] + d[1];\n                if (r &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; r &lt; rows &amp;&amp; c &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; c &lt; cols &amp;&amp; grid[r][c] != '#' &amp;&amp; dist[r][c] == -1) {\n                    dist[r][c] = dist[cur[0]][cur[1]] + 1;\n                    q.offer(new int[]{r, c});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return dist[rows - 1][cols - 1];\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = {1, 3, 5, 7};\n        System.out.println(lowerBound(a, 5) + \" \" + lowerBound(a, 9));\n        System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(a, 7) + \" \" + Arrays.binarySearch(a, 4));  // -(insertion)-1\n\n        char[][] grid = {\n            \"..#\".toCharArray(),\n            \".##\".toCharArray(),\n            \"...\".toCharArray(),\n        };\n        System.out.println(shortestPath(grid));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 4\n3 -3\n4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Sorting idioms and the subtraction trap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] intervals = {{5, 6}, {1, 3}, {2, 4}};\n        Arrays.sort(intervals, (x, y) -&gt; Integer.compare(x[0], y[0]));\n        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(intervals));\n\n        List&lt;String&gt; words = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(\"pear\", \"fig\", \"apple\", \"kiwi\"));\n        words.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length).thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()));\n        System.out.println(words);\n\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; nums = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, Integer.MIN_VALUE, 5));\n        nums.sort((x, y) -&gt; x - y);                  // BROKEN: x - y overflows\n        System.out.println(nums);\n        nums.sort(Integer::compare);                 // correct\n        System.out.println(nums);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[[1, 3], [2, 4], [5, 6]]\n[fig, kiwi, pear, apple]\n[1, 5, -2147483648]\n[-2147483648, 1, 5]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Handy one-liners</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Java</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Char ↔ index</td><td><code>c - 'a'</code>, <code>(char) ('a' + i)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Digit char → int</td><td><code>c - '0'</code> or <code>Character.getNumericValue(c)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Fill / copy arrays</td><td><code>Arrays.fill(a, -1)</code>, <code>Arrays.copyOfRange(a, l, r)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Sum / max of <code>int[]</code></td><td><code>Arrays.stream(a).sum()</code>, <code>.max().getAsInt()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> → <code>int[]</code></td><td><code>list.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Reverse a string</td><td><code>new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Infinity sentinels</td><td><code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code>, <code>Long.MAX_VALUE</code> (beware of <code>+ 1</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Bit tricks</td><td><code>Integer.bitCount(x)</code>, <code>x &amp; (x - 1)</code>, <code>1 &lt;&lt; k</code>, <code>x &gt;&gt;&gt; 1</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why ArrayDeque instead of Stack or LinkedList?</summary><div><p><code>Stack</code> extends the synchronized <code>Vector</code> (needless locking, and it exposes index access). <code>LinkedList</code> allocates a node per element and has poor cache locality. <code>ArrayDeque</code> is a resizable circular array: O(1) at both ends, fast and memory-compact — the Javadoc itself recommends it for stacks and queues. (It rejects <code>null</code> elements.)</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is (a, b) -&gt; a - b a bad comparator?</summary><div><p>The subtraction can overflow for large values of opposite sign (e.g. <code>1 - Integer.MIN_VALUE</code>), producing the wrong sign and an inconsistent ordering. Use <code>Integer.compare(a, b)</code> or <code>Comparator.comparingInt</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}